<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicholas of Cusa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1401–1464</span> · Also known as Nicolaus Cusanus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicholas of Cusa was a German philosopher, theologian and cardinal, author of On Learned Ignorance. A reformer at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a> who later sided with the pope, he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a> in 1450, where his efforts to recover church rights led to bitter conflict with Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-archduke-of-austria">Sigismund of Tyrol</a>, who imprisoned him in 1460.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-cusa.jpg" alt="Nicholas of Cusa" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Master of the Life of the Virgin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicholas_of_Cusa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
